<section id="s1" class="part">
<h2>Not yet read</h2>
<p>One item, new. The page holds only what you have not read. At 05:10 UTC on 6 November, you marked change 33 read, so the new item is numbered 34.</p>
<p>The skill said nothing about deleting branches. The curator gave an order in chat that nobody tries to delete a branch on GitHub, and that nobody asks him or reminds him to delete one. The skill now gives the workers that order. A cold read of the first wording found that it also forbade local deletions, and the skill writer fixed it. The item shows the net change of both rounds.</p>
<p>The item shows the text on <code>main</code> now, against the text before the first of the two commits. The words that were added are underlined. No words went.</p>

<section id="l34" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 34 &middot; committing.md, &ldquo;Pushing&rdquo;</span>No branch deleted on GitHub</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what was added; nothing was struck through</span>
<p class="where-in">committing.md, &ldquo;Pushing&rdquo;, line 41, a new bullet after the bullet on pushing a worker&rsquo;s own branch</p>
<ul><li>If you work on your own branch, push it: <code>git push -u origin &lt;branch&gt;</code> the first time, then <code>git push</code>. Your brief says how the branch reaches <code>main</code>.</li>
<li><span class="add">Do not try to delete a branch on GitHub: the automatic permission check refuses it, and the curator deletes it. Removing a worktree does not delete its branch, which stays. Ask nobody in your report to delete a branch.</span></li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule: the skill said nothing about deleting branches. But the session&rsquo;s automatic permission check refuses every deletion of a branch on GitHub from this container. The curator deletes those branches himself, when he gets to them, and he ordered in chat that nobody tries such a deletion, and that nobody asks him or reminds him to make one. So the bullet now tells a worker three things: it does not try to delete a branch on GitHub; when it removes a worktree, the worktree&rsquo;s branch stays; and its report asks nobody to delete a branch. The skill had no words on this before, so no words went. The cold read of the first wording found that it forbade every deletion, local ones too. But the record-landing workflow deletes its local branches, and those deletions work. So the fixed text forbids only a deletion on GitHub.</p></div>
</section>

